theory.intro:
Split $a\le x\le b$ into strips of width $\Delta x$ and build a rectangle on each, of height $f(x)$. Adding their areas gives the **Riemann sum** $\sum f(x)\Delta x$, an estimate of the area. The **definite integral** $\int_{a}^{b}f(x)\,dx$ is the limit of that sum as $\Delta x\to 0$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (2,1.909);
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) rectangle (3,2.338);
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) rectangle (4,2.700);
\fill[gray!35] (4,0) rectangle (5,3.019);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (2,1.909);
\draw (2,0) rectangle (3,2.338);
\draw (3,0) rectangle (4,2.700);
\draw (4,0) rectangle (5,3.019);
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (6.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:6,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.35*sqrt(\x)});
\node at (4.2,4) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[below] at (1,-0.12) {$a$};
\node[below] at (5,-0.12) {$b$};
\draw[<->] (3,-0.85) -- (4,-0.85) node[midway,below] {$\Delta x$};
\draw[<->] (5.55,0) -- (5.55,3.019) node[midway,right] {$f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, y=0.6cm]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,1) -- (3,7) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
