\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded region lies between the line and the $x$-axis.
Write the definite integral that gives its area.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) -- (1,1.25) -- plot[smooth,domain=1:4,variable=\x] ({\x},{0.25*\x*\x+1}) -- (4,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:4.5,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.25*\x*\x+1});
\node at (2.3,4.6) {$y=f(x)$};
\foreach \x in {1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5} \draw[dashed] (\x,0) -- (\x,{0.25*\x*\x+1});
\draw (1,0.15) -- (1,-0.15) node[below] {$1$};
\draw (4,0.15) -- (4,-0.15) node[below] {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The interval $1\le x\le 4$ has been divided into $6$ equal strips, shown by the dashed lines.
Find $\Delta x$, and state how many boundary points the $6$ strips have.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- plot[smooth,domain=0:2,variable=\x] ({\x},{\x*\x}) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,5.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:2.25,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x});
\node at (0.8,3.6) {$y=x^{2}$};
\foreach \x in {1,2} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded region is estimated by rectangles of width $\Delta x$.
Write $\lim_{\Delta x\to 0}\sum_{0}^{2}x^{2}\,\Delta x$ as a definite integral.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,-4) -- (3,-1) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-5.2) -- (0,1.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-0.8,-4.8) -- (4.4,0.4);
\node at (3.2,-2.4) {$y=x-4$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-1,-2,-3,-4} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded region lies entirely below the $x$-axis.
Is $\int_{0}^{3}(x-4)\,dx$ positive, negative or zero?
Give a reason.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, y=0.4cm]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,3) -- (4,11) -- (4,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.6,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$t$ (s)};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,14) node[above] {$v$ (m/s)};
\draw[thick,->] (0,3) -- (5.2,13.4);
\node at (1.6,10.5) {$v=2t+3$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} \draw (\x,0.4) -- (\x,-0.4) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {3,6,9,12} \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph shows the speed $v$ metres per second of a trolley over $0\le t\le 4$ seconds.
State what $\sum_{0}^{4}v\,\Delta t$ estimates, and give its units.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (-1,0) -- (-1,3) -- plot[smooth,domain=-1:2,variable=\x] ({\x},{4-\x*\x}) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-3.2,0) -- (3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.8) -- (0,5.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-2.35:2.35,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{4-\x*\x});
\node at (2.0,3.4) {$y=4-x^{2}$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded region is the area measured by $\int_{-1}^{2}(4-x^{2})\,dx$.
State the lower limit, the upper limit and the integrand.

